// apps/rtq: ray and point queries against a set of triangles, as Embree and
// FCPW answer them.
//
// The program says what is computed: the nearest triangle a ray hits
// (`trace`: Embree's rtcIntersect1, FCPW's Scene::intersect), whether it
// hits any (`occluded`: rtcOccluded1, intersect with checkForOcclusion) and
// the triangle closest to a point (`closest`: rtcPointQuery with Embree's
// closest-point tutorial's callback, FCPW's findClosestPoint), over a set of
// triangles. How that is computed -- that the set is Embree's BVH8 over
// Triangle4 leaves or FCPW's Mbvh over packets of sixteen, that a node's
// children are tested at the node and descended into nearest first, that
// the traversal is a loop over a stack -- is the schedule's
// (schedules/*.bonsai), and the tree and its bytes are the layout's
// (schedules/trees/*.bonsai). The driver (rtq_hook.cpp) builds the tree with
// the reference's own builder, hands the same rays and points to both, and
// compares answers and times.
//
// The arithmetic is the reference's, transcribed, and lives in a file of
// its own compiled beside this one: metrics/embree.bonsai has Embree's slab
// test, its Moeller-Trumbore test and its tutorial's closest point on a
// triangle; metrics/fcpw.bonsai has FCPW's slab test, its Moeller-Trumbore
// and its wide closest point, with its approximate reciprocals and its root
// per lane. A comparison against a reference runs the reference's own
// computation under the schedule and the layout, so that what the table
// measures is the compiler's traversal against the hand-written one and
// not one algorithm against another. Both files define, over the elements
// of elements.bonsai (the ray, the triangle, the box, the point, the set
// of triangles -- compiled first, so that the metric's answer records are
// declared over them and this file's exports over those), `intersects`,
// `distmin`, `distmax` and `contains` for a ray and a box, a ray and a
// triangle, a point and a box, and a point and a triangle, and the
// reference's answer records (`Hit`, `ClosestHit`) with the functions that
// fill them.

// Embree: TravRay -- the ray as the traversal reads it, its segment clamped
// to [0, inf) at entry (`tnear = max(tnear, 0)`, `tfar = max(tfar, 0)`,
// node_intersector1.h). A box's entry distance is `max(.., tnear)`, so with
// the clamp it is never negative, and the sort's key can be ordered as its
// bits without the sign flip that a negative float would need: Embree's
// `distance_i` is `asInt(tNear)` for the same reason; and the traversal's
// running best can be folded into `tfar` on the bits (SSA/Simplify.cpp)
// because the best is a non-negative number. The compiler carries the facts
// from here through the calls to the key and the node test
// (SSA/ConstantIntervals.h, SSA/SortRecursion.cpp), and for that the clamp
// has to leave a non-negative number: Embree's `max(tnear, 0)` is std::max,
// which hands a NaN or a negative zero through unchanged, and Embree rests
// instead on asserting the ray valid (`ray.valid()`: `tnear >= 0`). The
// clamp here is the same for every such ray and says so to the compiler:
// what is above zero stays, everything else -- negative, negative zero, not
// a number -- is zero.
func clamped(r : Ray) -> Ray = Ray{r.o, select(r.tnear > 0.0, r.tnear, 0.0), r.d,
                                  select(r.tfar > 0.0, r.tfar, 0.0)};

// Embree: rtcIntersect1; FCPW: Scene::intersect -- the nearest triangle the
// ray hits, if any, with the reference's record of the hit. The map
// annotates every candidate with `hit_record(r, t)`, the record of the same
// test `intersects` names and `distmin` keys, so the hit the argmin answers
// carries its record: the three ask one question of a candidate, the leaf
// evaluates them together and CSE keeps one test, and the argmin holds the
// record by value beside its reference to the triangle. That is where
// Embree makes its record too (MoellerTrumboreHitM::finalize in the leaf,
// for each hit nearer than the best), and where FCPW makes its point,
// normal and uv (for every lane of every packet) -- not a second test on
// the winner after the traversal, which cost 6-18% on primary rays. The
// same shape as apps/pbrt's trace.
func trace(r0 : Ray) -> option[(Triangle, Hit)] {
    r = clamped(r0);
    return argmin(|t : Triangle, h : Hit| distmin(r, t),
                  filter(|t : Triangle, h : Hit| intersects(r, t),
                         map(|t : Triangle| (t, hit_record(r, t)), triangles)));
}

// Embree: rtcOccluded1; FCPW: Scene::intersect with checkForOcclusion --
// whether the ray hits anything at all.
func occluded(r0 : Ray) -> bool {
    r = clamped(r0);
    return any(|t : Triangle| intersects(r, t), triangles);
}

// Embree: rtcPointQuery with the closest-point tutorial's callback; FCPW:
// Scene::findClosestPoint -- the triangle closest to the point. Both walk
// the tree ordered by the children's distance and shrink a radius to each
// closer triangle found; the argmin here is that search, its running best
// the radius, and the lowering prunes by it as both do: a child is entered
// only where its box is nearer than the best, and the best is lowered to
// the farthest a child's box can be, which FCPW does at every node
// (enqueueNodes) and Embree's callback form cannot. Every triangle the set
// holds is a candidate: which stored slots are triangles is the layout's
// to say, not this query's. The map annotates each candidate with the
// reference's record (the closest point, its distance), the same
// computation `distmin` keys, so one evaluation serves both, as for trace.
func closest(q : Point) -> option[(Triangle, ClosestHit)] {
    return argmin(|t : Triangle, c : ClosestHit| distmin(q, t),
                  map(|t : Triangle| (t, closest_record(q, t)), triangles));
}

// A batch of rays, each answered with the reference's hit record -- the
// triangle's id (0xffffffff for none, RTC_INVALID_GEOMETRY_ID) and what the
// reference writes beside it: Embree's t, u, v and Ng (RTCRayHit), FCPW's
// distance, point, normal and uv (Interaction); `Hit`, `hit_record` and
// `no_hit` are the metric file's, since the fields and their arithmetic are
// the reference's -- a batch answered with 1 where something is in the way,
// and a batch of points, each answered with the closest triangle, the point
// on it and the distance (`ClosestHit`, `closest_record`, `no_closest`, the
// metric file's again). The record is made once per query, for the element
// the argmin settled on, after the traversal; the references make theirs in
// the leaf for each candidate that improved on the best. The rays are a
// parfor, which a schedule may spread over the cores; the comparison's
// schedule leaves it unbound, a plain loop, since the measurement is
// single-threaded on both sides.
func [[export]] trace_all(n : u32, rays : array[Ray, n], hits : mut array[Hit, n]) {
    parfor i in 0u:n {
        h = trace(rays[i]);
        if h {
            found = *h;
            hits[i] = found[1];
        } else {
            hits[i] = no_hit();
        }
    }
    return;
}

func [[export]] occluded_all(n : u32, rays : array[Ray, n], hits : mut array[u32, n]) {
    parfor i in 0u:n {
        hits[i] = select(occluded(rays[i]), 1u, 0u);
    }
    return;
}

func [[export]] closest_all(n : u32, points : array[Point, n], hits : mut array[ClosestHit, n]) {
    parfor i in 0u:n {
        h = closest(points[i]);
        if h {
            found = *h;
            hits[i] = found[1];
        } else {
            hits[i] = no_closest();
        }
    }
    return;
}
